==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*kattōn}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|katte}}, {{cog|osx|katta}}, {{cog|dum|katte}} ({{cog|nl|kat}}), {{cog|goh|kazza}} ({{etyl|de|-}} [[Katze]]), {{cog|non|ketta}} ({{cog|sv|katta}}). The word existed in the Germanic languages in a masculine gender also, represented in Old English by {{m|ang|catt}}. The word appears to be related to {{cog|LL.|cattus}} as well as to similar words in the Slavic and Celtic languages, but the ultimate source is uncertain. See {{m|en|cat}} for more.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈkɑt.te/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|f}}

# a female [[cat]].

